WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:06.740
"Plant-Based vs. Meat-Based Meals
for Weight Loss by Boosting GLP-1"

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:08.408
Vegetarian diets appear to offer

00:00:08.408 --> 00:00:10.711
significant benefits
for weight reduction.

00:00:10.711 --> 00:00:13.680
Might the appetite-suppressing
hormone GLP-1

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.716
have anything to do with that?

00:00:15.716 --> 00:00:17.618
Let’s compare
the GLP-1 inducing effects

00:00:17.618 --> 00:00:20.187
of a meaty meal
versus a plant-based one,

00:00:20.187 --> 00:00:21.321
with the same
number of calories

00:00:21.321 --> 00:00:24.391
on gastrointestinal
hormone responses.

00:00:24.391 --> 00:00:26.026
I mean, we know that
the intake of meat,

00:00:26.026 --> 00:00:27.060
particularly processed meat,

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.563
is a dietary risk factor
for diabetes.

00:00:29.563 --> 00:00:31.665
Meat intake impairs
insulin sensitivity

00:00:31.665 --> 00:00:34.101
and leads to increased
oxidative stress.

00:00:34.101 --> 00:00:35.435
However, its effect
on the secretion

00:00:35.435 --> 00:00:39.339
of after-meal gut hormones,
like GLP-1, has been unclear.

00:00:39.339 --> 00:00:42.809
So, what happens when people
either with or without diabetes

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:45.045
eat a pork burger with cheese

00:00:45.045 --> 00:00:48.916
versus a plant-based burger
made with couscous and oats?

00:00:48.916 --> 00:00:51.818
For those with diabetes,
there was a doubling of GLP-1

00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:53.987
half an hour after
the plant-based burger

00:00:53.987 --> 00:00:56.156
was eaten compared
to the meat burger.

00:00:56.156 --> 00:00:57.791
But if you compare
the two meals,

00:00:57.791 --> 00:00:59.626
although they were
matched for calories,

00:00:59.626 --> 00:01:01.561
they were not matched
for macronutrients.

00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:03.397
The meaty meal
had more protein and fat,

00:01:03.397 --> 00:01:04.965
fewer carbohydrates,

00:01:04.965 --> 00:01:08.168
and since different macros
can affect GLP-1 differently,

00:01:08.168 --> 00:01:10.470
the difference
in GLP-1 secretion may have

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:13.240
had more to do with the macros
than the meat.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:15.509
Okay, so how about using
a tofu burger

00:01:15.509 --> 00:01:18.278
that’s macronutrient matched?

00:01:18.278 --> 00:01:20.547
People with diabetes had
a significantly higher increase

00:01:20.547 --> 00:01:23.116
in GLP-1 release
after the veggie meal,

00:01:23.116 --> 00:01:25.786
compared to the meat meal,
just like in the last study.

00:01:25.786 --> 00:01:27.087
But if you remember,
in the last study,

00:01:27.087 --> 00:01:29.656
the veggie meal only seemed
to boost GLP-1 secretion

00:01:29.656 --> 00:01:31.625
in those with diabetes.

00:01:31.625 --> 00:01:34.394
Was that because of
the macro mismatch as well?

00:01:34.394 --> 00:01:37.230
There’s only one way
to find out.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.166
A randomized crossover trial
tested the effects

00:01:39.166 --> 00:01:41.768
of two meals matched in calories
and macronutrients

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:43.837
using the same tofu burger

00:01:43.837 --> 00:01:45.605
versus
a conventional meat burger,

00:01:45.605 --> 00:01:46.773
and compared to the meat meal,

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:49.876
the vegan meal boosted GLP-1
levels by 42 percent

00:01:49.876 --> 00:01:52.913
in those with diabetes
and was about just as effective

00:01:52.913 --> 00:01:56.016
in boosting GLP-1
in those without diabetes.

00:01:56.016 --> 00:01:59.686
Does that actually translate
into improving satiety, though?

00:01:59.686 --> 00:02:02.022
Yes:
"A plant-based meal increases

00:02:02.022 --> 00:02:04.324
gastrointestinal hormones
and satiety

00:02:04.324 --> 00:02:07.194
more than an energy-
and-macronutrient-matched

00:02:07.194 --> 00:02:09.296
processed-meat meal
in type 2 diabetic,

00:02:09.296 --> 00:02:11.765
obese, and healthy men."

00:02:11.765 --> 00:02:13.567
Satiety was greater
in all participants

00:02:13.567 --> 00:02:15.836
after the vegan meal
compared to the meaty one.

00:02:15.836 --> 00:02:18.338
And since enhancing satiety
is one of the major challenges

00:02:18.338 --> 00:02:21.441
in the dietary treatment
of obesity and type 2 diabetes,

00:02:21.441 --> 00:02:23.510
plant-based meals
may be an effective strategy

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:25.746
in solving this problem.

00:02:25.746 --> 00:02:29.416
But does this actually translate
into eating less?

00:02:29.416 --> 00:02:31.284
Researchers had
study participants consume

00:02:31.284 --> 00:02:33.754
a pasta lunch
at an all-you-can-eat buffet

00:02:33.754 --> 00:02:35.956
with either
ground plant-based meat

00:02:35.956 --> 00:02:37.724
versus ground beef,

00:02:37.724 --> 00:02:39.860
and participants consumed
significantly fewer calories

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:42.662
of the pasta with
the plant-based burger meat.

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:45.799
Yet, despite eating less,
they felt just as satisfied,

00:02:45.799 --> 00:02:47.768
full, and satiated.

00:02:47.768 --> 00:02:50.070
But this was unlikely
to be a GLP-1 effect,

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:52.706
since rather than GLP-1
driving lower food intake,

00:02:52.706 --> 00:02:56.209
lower food intake appeared
to drive lower GLP-1.

00:02:56.209 --> 00:02:58.278
If you look at the effects
of mycoprotein,

00:02:58.278 --> 00:03:01.014
which make up
meat substitutes like Quorn,

00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:04.151
made from the mushroom kingdom
rather than the plant kingdom,

00:03:04.151 --> 00:03:06.953
mycoprotein not only shows
a decrease in calorie intake

00:03:06.953 --> 00:03:08.155
at an all-you-can-eat meal,

00:03:08.155 --> 00:03:10.824
but so suppresses
subsequent appetite

00:03:10.824 --> 00:03:14.061
that there’s a decrease
in intake over the entire day.

00:03:14.061 --> 00:03:16.563
When researchers compared it
to eating chicken,

00:03:16.563 --> 00:03:18.865
they saw a significant
decreased calorie intake

00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:20.500
during the mycoprotein meal

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:22.769
that was not compensated for
later in the day,

00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:24.838
which led to an average
caloric reduction

00:03:24.838 --> 00:03:27.641
over the whole day
by 188 calories.

00:03:27.641 --> 00:03:31.478
And, this was accomplished
without altering GLP-1.

00:03:31.478 --> 00:03:34.147
So, GLP-1 may not be the reason

00:03:34.147 --> 00:03:36.016
plant-based meals
tend to be more satiating

00:03:36.016 --> 00:03:38.185
or why whole food
plant-based diets

00:03:38.185 --> 00:03:41.955
apparently achieve greater
weight loss than any other diet.

00:03:41.955 --> 00:03:43.290
Maybe it’s because
plant-based diets

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:46.626
are associated with
an improved gut microbiome,

00:03:46.626 --> 00:03:49.129
increased insulin sensitivity,
better metabolism,

00:03:49.129 --> 00:03:51.264
reduced caloric density.

00:03:51.264 --> 00:03:53.834
Dietary fiber alone
can improve your microbiome

00:03:53.834 --> 00:03:56.837
and reduce calorie density,
and a few types of fiber

00:03:56.837 --> 00:03:59.806
may be able
to also boost GLP-1.

00:03:59.806 --> 00:04:02.709
I’ll cover that, next.

